# Recovering a volatility

[Back to Implied Volatility](../finance_02_implied_volatility.md)

Option markets quote prices, but traders compare options by their *implied volatility*: the value of σ that makes the Black-Scholes price equal the observed price. Because the Black-Scholes price rises strictly with σ, that value is unique when it exists, and `implied_vol` finds it. The programs below quote the prices of Black-Scholes fields and invert them with a helper, `bs_iv`, that builds a one-option field and passes the price as the quote; the volatility of that field plays no part.

```cpp
Series module::Field::implied_vol(const pricing::ImpliedVolOptions &options = {}, Config cfg = {}) const;                          // the smile the field's own model implies
Series module::Field::implied_vol(const Series &quotes, const pricing::ImpliedVolOptions &options = {}, Config cfg = {}) const;    // the volatilities market quotes imply
```

`implied_vol` is a method of the [`Field`](../finance_01_black_scholes.md), not a separate engine: the field supplies the instrument (type, strikes, maturities) and the market (spot, rate, dividend yield), and the method inverts one Black-Scholes price per option. With no argument it inverts the prices the field's *own* model produces, which turns any model, Heston or SABR as well as Black-Scholes, into a smile of implied volatilities; with `quotes` it inverts observed prices. The result is a `Series` with one volatility per option, or `NaN` where no volatility in the search range reproduces the price ([Controls and the no-arbitrage bounds](controls_and_the_no_arbitrage_bounds.md)).

## A round trip

The program prices calls and puts at volatilities from 5% to 200%, at three strikes, and recovers each volatility from its price. The largest error over all of them is reported.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace prc = pluss::subsp::math::finance::pricing;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;

// One Black-Scholes option as a field: instrument, market and model, priced by the analytic engine.
mod::Field bs_field(prc::OptionType type, double S, double K, double r, double q, double T, double vol) {
  return mod::Field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(type, K, T),
                    .market = fin::market::make(S, r, q),
                    .model = fin::model::BlackScholes{.volatility = pluss::make_series({vol})},
                    .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Analytic}};
}
double bs_price(prc::OptionType type, double S, double K, double r, double q, double T, double vol) {
  return bs_field(type, S, K, r, q, T, vol).price().price.items<double>()[0];
}


// Implied volatility of one quote: a Black-Scholes field whose own volatility is irrelevant, because the quote is passed in.
double bs_iv(prc::OptionType type, double price, double S, double K, double r, double q, double T, const prc::ImpliedVolOptions &options = {}) {
  return bs_field(type, S, K, r, q, T, 0.2).implied_vol(pluss::make_series({price}), options).items<double>()[0];
}
// Implied volatilities of a strip of call quotes with their own spots and maturities.
pluss::Series iv_strip(const pluss::Series &quotes, const pluss::Series &spots, const pluss::Series &maturities, double K, double r, double q) {
  mod::Field strip{.instrument = {.type = prc::Call, .strike = pluss::make_series({K}), .maturity = maturities},
                   .market = {.spot = spots, .rate = pluss::make_series({r}), .dividend_yield = pluss::make_series({q})},
                   .model = fin::model::BlackScholes{.volatility = pluss::make_series({0.2})}};
  return strip.implied_vol(quotes);
}

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.sci(2));
  const double spot = 100.0, rate = 0.03, dividend = 0.01, maturity = 0.5;
  double worst = 0.0, worst_vol = 0.0, worst_strike = 0.0, worst_price = 0.0;
  int recovered = 0, total = 0;
  for (auto type : {prc::Call, prc::Put}) {
    for (double strike : {80.0, 100.0, 125.0}) {
      for (double vol : {0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.4, 0.8, 1.5, 2.0}) {
        const double price = bs_price(type, spot, strike, rate, dividend, maturity, vol);
        const double iv = bs_iv(type, price, spot, strike, rate, dividend, maturity);
        ++total;
        if (std::isfinite(iv)) {
          ++recovered;
          if (std::abs(iv - vol) > worst) {
            worst = std::abs(iv - vol);
            worst_vol = vol;
            worst_strike = strike;
            worst_price = price;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  out.writeln("recovered", recovered, "of", total, " largest error", worst, " (volatility", worst_vol, "strike", worst_strike, "price", worst_price, ")");
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(8));
  const double price = bs_price(prc::Call, spot, 100.0, rate, dividend, maturity, 0.2345);
  out.writeln("one price", price, " implies", bs_iv(prc::Call, price, spot, 100.0, rate, dividend, maturity));
}
```

All 42 prices, calls and puts at three strikes and seven volatilities, return a volatility. The largest error is 7.9 × 10⁻⁶, for the call at strike 80 and a volatility of 5%: its price, 20.7, is almost entirely intrinsic value, and its time value is so small that the price, at the precision of a double, pins the volatility down only to about the sixth decimal. Everywhere else the error is far smaller. The single price 7.0488 implies 0.2345, exactly the volatility that produced it.

## The five methods

`ImpliedVolOptions::method` selects the root finder: `Newton` (using the vega as the derivative), `Bisection` (slow and certain), `Secant`, `Brent` (the default, with `Auto` an alias for it), each safeguarded so that it stays inside the bracket [`lower_vol`, `upper_vol`]. All five solve the same equation, so they agree to the tolerance. The program recovers a volatility from a normal price, from a nearly worthless price (deep out of the money, short maturity, where the vega is tiny and the price is almost flat in σ) and from a deep in-the-money price.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace prc = pluss::subsp::math::finance::pricing;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;

// One Black-Scholes option as a field: instrument, market and model, priced by the analytic engine.
mod::Field bs_field(prc::OptionType type, double S, double K, double r, double q, double T, double vol) {
  return mod::Field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(type, K, T),
                    .market = fin::market::make(S, r, q),
                    .model = fin::model::BlackScholes{.volatility = pluss::make_series({vol})},
                    .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Analytic}};
}
double bs_price(prc::OptionType type, double S, double K, double r, double q, double T, double vol) {
  return bs_field(type, S, K, r, q, T, vol).price().price.items<double>()[0];
}


// Implied volatility of one quote: a Black-Scholes field whose own volatility is irrelevant, because the quote is passed in.
double bs_iv(prc::OptionType type, double price, double S, double K, double r, double q, double T, const prc::ImpliedVolOptions &options = {}) {
  return bs_field(type, S, K, r, q, T, 0.2).implied_vol(pluss::make_series({price}), options).items<double>()[0];
}
// Implied volatilities of a strip of call quotes with their own spots and maturities.
pluss::Series iv_strip(const pluss::Series &quotes, const pluss::Series &spots, const pluss::Series &maturities, double K, double r, double q) {
  mod::Field strip{.instrument = {.type = prc::Call, .strike = pluss::make_series({K}), .maturity = maturities},
                   .market = {.spot = spots, .rate = pluss::make_series({r}), .dividend_yield = pluss::make_series({q})},
                   .model = fin::model::BlackScholes{.volatility = pluss::make_series({0.2})}};
  return strip.implied_vol(quotes);
}

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.sci(2));
  struct Case {
    const char *label;
    prc::OptionType type;
    double strike, maturity, vol;
  };
  const std::vector<Case> cases{{"at the money, 1 year        ", prc::Call, 100.0, 1.0, 0.3},
                                {"deep out of the money, 2 weeks", prc::Call, 160.0, 0.04, 0.3},
                                {"deep in the money, 1 year     ", prc::Call, 40.0, 1.0, 0.3},
                                {"put out of the money          ", prc::Put, 70.0, 0.5, 0.45}};
  const std::vector<std::pair<const char *, prc::ImpliedVolMethod>> methods{
      {"Auto", prc::Auto}, {"Newton", prc::Newton}, {"Bisection", prc::Bisection}, {"Secant", prc::Secant}, {"Brent", prc::Brent}};
  out.writeln("error of the recovered volatility, by method:");
  for (const auto &c : cases) {
    const double price = bs_price(c.type, 100.0, c.strike, 0.02, 0.0, c.maturity, c.vol);
    std::vector<double> errors;
    for (const auto &[name, method] : methods) {
      prc::ImpliedVolOptions options;
      options.method = method;
      errors.push_back(std::abs(bs_iv(c.type, price, 100.0, c.strike, 0.02, 0.0, c.maturity, options) - c.vol));
    }
    out.writeln(c.label, " price", price, errors);
  }
}
```

All five methods recover the volatility to 10⁻⁸ or better in every case, including the price of 2.5 × 10⁻¹⁵ of an option 60% out of the money two weeks from expiry, where the price is nearly flat in the volatility. Newton and Secant use the vega or a secant to reach the answer in a few steps, Brent takes a similar number, and bisection reaches the tolerance (6 × 10⁻⁹) by halving.

## Many prices at once

With a strip, each element is solved independently, and an element with no solution is `NaN` without affecting the others. The program recovers the volatilities of a path of one-month option prices, as the spot and the remaining maturity change, and then inserts an impossible price.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace prc = pluss::subsp::math::finance::pricing;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;

// One Black-Scholes option as a field: instrument, market and model, priced by the analytic engine.
mod::Field bs_field(prc::OptionType type, double S, double K, double r, double q, double T, double vol) {
  return mod::Field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(type, K, T),
                    .market = fin::market::make(S, r, q),
                    .model = fin::model::BlackScholes{.volatility = pluss::make_series({vol})},
                    .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Analytic}};
}
double bs_price(prc::OptionType type, double S, double K, double r, double q, double T, double vol) {
  return bs_field(type, S, K, r, q, T, vol).price().price.items<double>()[0];
}


// Implied volatility of one quote: a Black-Scholes field whose own volatility is irrelevant, because the quote is passed in.
double bs_iv(prc::OptionType type, double price, double S, double K, double r, double q, double T, const prc::ImpliedVolOptions &options = {}) {
  return bs_field(type, S, K, r, q, T, 0.2).implied_vol(pluss::make_series({price}), options).items<double>()[0];
}
// Implied volatilities of a strip of call quotes with their own spots and maturities.
pluss::Series iv_strip(const pluss::Series &quotes, const pluss::Series &spots, const pluss::Series &maturities, double K, double r, double q) {
  mod::Field strip{.instrument = {.type = prc::Call, .strike = pluss::make_series({K}), .maturity = maturities},
                   .market = {.spot = spots, .rate = pluss::make_series({r}), .dividend_yield = pluss::make_series({q})},
                   .model = fin::model::BlackScholes{.volatility = pluss::make_series({0.2})}};
  return strip.implied_vol(quotes);
}

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const double strike = 100.0, rate = 0.03, dividend = 0.0;
  const std::vector<double> path{100.0, 100.8, 99.9, 101.5, 102.4, 101.1};
  std::vector<double> remaining, true_vol{0.22, 0.24, 0.23, 0.21, 0.20, 0.25};
  for (int day = 0; day < 6; ++day) {
    remaining.push_back((30.0 - day) / 365.0);
  }
  const auto spot = pluss::make_series(path, {6}, as_f64());
  const auto ttm = pluss::make_series(remaining, {6}, as_f64());

  // Each element is priced at its own volatility, then inverted.
  std::vector<double> prices;
  for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) {
    prices.push_back(bs_price(prc::Call, path[i], strike, rate, dividend, remaining[i], true_vol[i]));
  }
  const auto price_series = pluss::make_series(prices, {6}, as_f64());
  out.writeln("recovered", iv_strip(price_series, spot, ttm, strike, rate, dividend).items<double>());
  out.writeln("true     ", true_vol);

  // With one maturity for every element, the maturity is a scalar.
  out.writeln("fixed maturity of 30 days:", iv_strip(price_series, spot, pluss::make_series({30.0 / 365.0}), strike, rate, dividend).items<double>());

  // One impossible price (below the intrinsic value) yields NaN for that element only.
  prices[3] = 0.0;
  out.writeln("with a zero price in the fourth element:", iv_strip(pluss::make_series(prices, {6}, as_f64()), spot, ttm, strike, rate, dividend).items<double>());
}
```

Each element is solved independently and returns its own volatility, 0.22, 0.24, 0.23, 0.21, 0.20 and 0.25, using its own remaining maturity. The scalar-maturity form applies the same 30 days to every element, so it returns different volatilities (0.2356 for the second): the remaining maturity matters. A zero price in the fourth element gives `NaN` there and leaves the others unchanged.

